Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage
• rental.rental_id   : unique identifier of each rental order – used for counting totals  
• rental.rental_date  : timestamp when the order was created – used to extract the year (strftime('%Y', …)) and month (strftime('%m', …))  
• rental.staff_id    : employee who created the order – used to join with staff to know the store  
• staff.staff_id     : primary key for staff – join key to rental  
• staff.store_id     : store in which the employee works – used for grouping results by store  
• store.store_id    : store identifier – handy if you want to join to the store table for extra columns (not strictly required here)  

Typical conditions that could be useful  
• WHERE staff.store_id IS NOT NULL  – make sure the rental is associated with a store  
• WHERE rental.rental_date IS NOT NULL – ensure the date exists  
• Optional: add a date range, e.g. WHERE rental.rental_date >= '2005‑01‑01' if you want to restrict the analysis period  

Below are ten incremental SQLite SELECT queries (each limited to 20 rows) that move from simple inspection to the final answer.  

```sql
-- 1. Description: List distinct store IDs that have staff members.
SELECT DISTINCT s.store_id
FROM staff AS s
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Inspect the different years present in rental data.
SELECT DISTINCT strftime('%Y', r.rental_date) AS rental_year
FROM rental AS r
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Inspect the different months present in rental data.
SELECT DISTINCT strftime('%m', r.rental_date) AS rental_month
FROM rental AS r
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Count rentals per store (via staff) without breaking down by date yet.
SELECT s.store_id,
       COUNT(*) AS total_rentals
FROM   rental AS r
JOIN   staff  AS s ON s.staff_id = r.staff_id
GROUP  BY s.store_id
ORDER  BY total_rentals DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Count rentals per store, year and month.
SELECT s.store_id,
       strftime('%Y', r.rental_date) AS rental_year,
       strftime('%m', r.rental_date) AS rental_month,
       COUNT(*)                     AS total_rentals
FROM   rental AS r
JOIN   staff  AS s ON s.staff_id = r.staff_id
GROUP  BY s.store_id,
         rental_year,
         rental_month
ORDER  BY s.store_id,
         total_rentals DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Same as #5 but ordered to see the peak months first for every store.
SELECT s.store_id,
       strftime('%Y', r.rental_date) AS rental_year,
       strftime('%m', r.rental_date) AS rental_month,
       COUNT(*)                     AS total_rentals
FROM   rental AS r
JOIN   staff  AS s ON s.staff_id = r.staff_id
GROUP  BY s.store_id,
         rental_year,
         rental_month
ORDER  BY s.store_id,
         total_rentals DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Use a window function to rank months inside each store by rental volume.
SELECT store_id,
       rental_year,
       rental_month,
       total_rentals,
       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY total_rentals DESC) AS rn
FROM (
    SELECT s.store_id                               AS store_id,
           strftime('%Y', r.rental_date)            AS rental_year,
           strftime('%m', r.rental_date)            AS rental_month,
           COUNT(*)                                 AS total_rentals
    FROM   rental AS r
    JOIN   staff  AS s ON s.staff_id = r.staff_id
    GROUP  BY s.store_id,
             rental_year,
             rental_month
) AS x
ORDER BY store_id, rn
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Return only the top‑ranked (highest rentals) month per store with the window function.
SELECT store_id,
       rental_year,
       rental_month,
       total_rentals
FROM (
    SELECT store_id,
           rental_year,
           rental_month,
           total_rentals,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY total_rentals DESC) AS rn
    FROM (
        SELECT s.store_id                            AS store_id,
               strftime('%Y', r.rental_date)         AS rental_year,
               strftime('%m', r.rental_date)         AS rental_month,
               COUNT(*)                              AS total_rentals
        FROM   rental AS r
        JOIN   staff  AS s ON s.staff_id = r.staff_id
        GROUP  BY s.store_id,
                 rental_year,
                 rental_month
    )
)
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY store_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Achieve the same result using a correlated sub‑query (alternative technique).
SELECT a.store_id,
       a.rental_year,
       a.rental_month,
       a.total_rentals
FROM (
      SELECT s.store_id                            AS store_id,
             strftime('%Y', r.rental_date)         AS rental_year,
             strftime('%m', r.rental_date)         AS rental_month,
             COUNT(*)                              AS total_rentals
      FROM   rental AS r
      JOIN   staff  AS s ON s.staff_id = r.staff_id
      GROUP  BY s.store_id,
               rental_year,
               rental_month
) AS a
WHERE a.total_rentals = (
      SELECT MAX(b.total_rentals)
      FROM (
            SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_rentals
            FROM   rental AS r2
            JOIN   staff  AS s2 ON s2.staff_id = r2.staff_id
            WHERE  s2.store_id = a.store_id
            GROUP  BY strftime('%Y', r2.rental_date),
                     strftime('%m', r2.rental_date)
      ) AS b
)
ORDER BY a.store_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final concise answer – the year and month with the highest rental orders
--                   for each store, plus the total number of rentals in that period.
SELECT store_id,
       rental_year,
       rental_month,
       total_rentals
FROM (
    SELECT s.store_id                            AS store_id,
           strftime('%Y', r.rental_date)         AS rental_year,
           strftime('%m', r.rental_date)         AS rental_month,
           COUNT(*)                              AS total_rentals,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY s.store_id
                              ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rn
    FROM   rental AS r
    JOIN   staff  AS s ON s.staff_id = r.staff_id
    GROUP  BY s.store_id,
             rental_year,
             rental_month
)
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY store_id
LIMIT 20;
```

The last query returns exactly what was asked:  
• store_id – the store’s identifier  
• rental_year – the year with the maximum rentals for that store  
• rental_month – the corresponding month (two‑digit: ‘01’ = January, … ‘12’ = December)  
• total_rentals – how many rentals were created by that store’s staff during that year‑month  

(If you prefer month names, replace strftime('%m', …) with strftime('%m', …) || ' – ' || strftime('%Y', …) or use CASE expressions.)
